<TopicPage slug="subjects/earth-science/foundational/earth-interior">

  <h2>Where you are, and what you will learn</h2>
  <p>
    This lesson takes you on a journey no human has ever made: from the surface to the centre
    of the Earth, 6,371 kilometres straight down. You need nothing from earlier lessons —
    only arithmetic (averages and ratios) and everyday physics (hot things rise, pressure
    squeezes). Everything else is built here, step by step.
  </p>
  <p>By the end you will be able to:</p>
  <ul>
    <li>explain how the interior is mapped without a single direct observation — with seismic waves, gravity and meteorites;</li>
    <li>slice the planet two ways — by chemical composition (crust, mantle, core) and by mechanical behaviour (lithosphere, asthenosphere and below) — and say why both schemes exist;</li>
    <li>read the geotherm, the graph of temperature against depth, and explain its kinks;</li>
    <li>retell the detective story that fixed the layer boundaries — Mohorovičić, Gutenberg and Lehmann;</li>
    <li>present Wegener's evidence for drifting continents and explain why it was rejected until seafloor spreading supplied the missing engine;</li>
    <li>predict what happens at divergent, convergent and transform boundaries, and at hot spots.</li>
  </ul>
  <p>
    One habit runs through the page: every claim about the deep Earth is traced to
    <em>evidence</em> — because the central skill of geology is knowing a great deal about a
    place nobody can visit.
  </p>

  <h2>A machine we have never seen</h2>
  <p>
    We live on the skin of a machine we have never visited. No drill has passed about 12 km:
    the Kola Superdeep Borehole in Russia reached 12,262 m in 1989 and still holds the record
    — a pinprick through a 6,371 km radius, like piercing an apple a fraction of a
    millimetre deep. Everything deeper is known the way doctors know hearts: by listening.
    When earthquakes ring through the planet, <strong>seismic waves</strong> — vibrations
    travelling through rock — speed up, slow down, bend or vanish depending on what they
    cross, and reading those signals from thousands of stations worldwide maps the interior
    in astonishing detail, down to slabs of old ocean floor sinking through the mantle today.
  </p>
  <p>
    The two kinds of body wave are easy to picture. A <strong>P wave</strong> ("primary" — it
    arrives first) is a push wave: give a Slinky a sharp shove and watch the compression run
    down its coils; P waves travel through solids <em>and</em> liquids. An <strong>S
    wave</strong> ("secondary") is a shear wave: flick a rope sideways and watch the wiggle
    run along it; S waves need a material that can spring back sideways, so they cross
    solids but are <em>stopped dead by liquid</em>. That one difference — P passes, S
    blocked — will shortly prove that part of Earth's core is liquid.
  </p>
  <p>
    Temperature is the second spy. The graph below is the <strong>geotherm</strong>: how hot
    it gets as you descend. The near-surface climb (roughly 15–30 °C per kilometre — miners
    have always known their pits get hot) steepens through the crust, then the curve's dips
    and kinks around 400 and 660 km are not pauses in heating but mineral phase changes,
    where olivine's atoms reorganise under pressure and briefly absorb energy. Read it to
    the bottom: the inner core sits near 5,400 °C — about the temperature of the Sun's
    visible surface. The coolest place on the whole journey is where you are.
  </p>

  <Widget no="1" caption="the geotherm — temperature from crust to core; note the dips around 400–660 km" widget="plotter" config={geothermCfg} />
  <p>
    <strong>What to notice:</strong> the curve never cools — every metre down is hotter —
    but its <em>steepness</em> changes. Steep in the lithosphere, nearly flat through the
    middle mantle (about 1,400–1,900 °C over 500-plus km), then steepening again near the
    core–mantle boundary at 2,890 km, where the curve jumps toward 3,800 °C. The gentle
    dip-and-recover around 400 and 660 km marks where pressure forces mantle minerals into
    denser crystal arrangements. And notice what the flatness means physically: heat is
    being carried upward faster than plain conduction allows — the mantle is convecting,
    stirring like a pot of soup where warmed material rises and cooler material sinks to
    replace it. That slow convection is the engine you will meet again at the end of this
    page.
  </p>
  <p>
    Why is the inside hot at all? Two sources contribute roughly equally. Half is
    <strong>primordial</strong> — frictional heat left over from the collisions that built
    the Earth, plus the heat released when gravity pulled the iron down to form the core.
    Half is <strong>radioactive decay</strong> — unstable isotopes of uranium, potassium and
    thorium, concentrated in the mantle, releasing energy as they decay. The radioactive
    share is slowly shrinking as the isotopes are used up (it is now roughly a quarter of
    what it was when Earth formed), so the interior is gradually cooling — small planets
    like Mercury and Mars, and our own Moon, have already cooled far enough that their
    mantles barely flow.
  </p>
  <p>
    <strong>So far:</strong> the deep Earth is mapped by listening — P and S waves — and by
    measuring temperature. The geotherm climbs from about 15 °C at your feet to about 5,400
    °C at the centre. Now we name what all that heat is cooking: the layers.
  </p>

  <h2>The layers, two ways of slicing</h2>
  <p>
    Geologists slice the planet twice, and keeping the two schemes apart is the key to the
    whole subject. By <em>chemical composition</em>: <strong>crust</strong>,
    <strong>mantle</strong>, <strong>core</strong>. By <em>mechanical behaviour</em> — rigid
    versus able to flow: <strong>lithosphere</strong>, <strong>asthenosphere</strong>, and
    the deeper zones. The schemes overlap but do not match, because behaviour depends not
    only on what rock is made of but on the pressure and temperature it sits at.
  </p>
  <p>
    Chemically: the crust is the thin skin, rich in silicon, aluminium and oxygen — ocean
    floors are 5–10 km of dense basalt, continents 30–70 km of thick, buoyant granite. The
    mantle, about 2,900 km thick and the largest layer by volume, is ultramafic rock —
    olivine-rich peridotite, with more iron and magnesium and less silicon than the crust —
    and it is <em>solid</em>, but solid that flows: like glass in old windows or asphalt on a
    hot day, it creeps a few centimetres per year. The core, about 3,500 km thick, is iron
    and nickel. The outer core is liquid — its churning generates Earth's magnetic field —
    and the inner core (from 5,150 km) is solid iron <em>hotter</em> than the liquid above
    it, yet crushed by pressure past iron's melting point. Pressure, not temperature alone,
    decides state.
  </p>
  <p>
    Mechanically: the <strong>lithosphere</strong> is the strong, rigid outer shell — crust
    plus the topmost mantle — broken into the moving tectonic plates. Beneath it, at about
    100–250 km, lies the <strong>asthenosphere</strong>, mantle rock held at temperatures so
    close to its melting point that the rock weakens and flows plastically; seismic waves
    slow down in it, which is why it is also called the low-velocity zone, and a small
    fraction of melt in mineral pores helps soften it. Below 660 km the rest of the mantle —
    sometimes called the <strong>mesosphere</strong> — is entirely solid and too strong to
    break, which is why no earthquake ever originates there. Almost all magma is born in or
    just above the asthenosphere, where rock is nearest to melting and needs only a nudge —
    added water or reduced pressure — to liquefy.
  </p>
  <p><em>Common misconception:</em> the mantle is a sea of molten rock with continents
  floating on it. Nearly all of the mantle is solid — rock hot enough to creep a few
  centimetres per year, which is not the same as liquid; the only liquid layer is the outer
  core, and magma is produced locally in small volumes. The plates glide on solid-but-flowing
  rock, not on an underground ocean of lava.</p>
  <p>
    A sanity check ties the layers to arithmetic: the whole Earth's average density is about
    5.5 g/cm³, while surface rocks are 2.7–3.0 — so the deep interior must be dense,
    iron-rich material. That simple weighing, before any seismology, is what told scientists
    the core was metal.
  </p>

  <h2>How the numbers were won</h2>
  <p>
    The seismological detective story ran in three famous steps. In 1909 the Croatian
    seismologist Andrija Mohorovičić noticed that stations far from an earthquake received
    two sets of waves seconds apart — and that the later-arriving set had gone <em>down</em>
    into faster rock and been bent back up, beating the direct route despite the longer
    journey. The speed jump marked the crust's base, the <strong>Moho</strong>: 30–40 km
    beneath most continents, 5–10 km beneath the oceans. In 1914 Beno Gutenberg fixed the
    core–mantle boundary near 2,890 km, and the <strong>S-wave shadow zone</strong> proved
    the outer core liquid: shear cannot cross liquid, so stations on the far side of the
    planet from a big quake — beyond about 103° of arc — hear no S waves at all, a ring of
    silence whose size reveals the liquid core's depth. In 1936 Inge Lehmann, studying the
    faint echoes that <em>did</em> arrive inside the P-wave shadow, discovered a solid inner
    core reflecting waves within the liquid one — a discovery made from a single Danish
    station and superb reasoning.
  </p>
  <p>
    Two more lines of evidence close the case. Waves reflect off boundaries like sonar
    echosounding, and arrival times now fix layer depths to within kilometres;
    <strong>seismic tomography</strong> — the same trick as a medical CT scan, run with
    earthquake waves — even images cold sinking slabs and hot rising plumes in three
    dimensions. And <strong>meteorites are samples</strong>: iron meteorites are fragments of
    shattered planetesimal cores, thought to resemble Earth's own core in composition (iron
    with nickel and heavier siderophile elements) — the only "core specimen" anyone will
    ever hold.
  </p>
  <p>
    <strong>So far:</strong> the architecture is known — crust, mantle, core, and the
    mechanical zones between — and it was all won by listening. But knowing the machine's
    shape raised the question that would convulse geology for half a century: does the
    surface of this machine move?
  </p>

  <h2>Wegener's drifting continents</h2>
  <p>
    In 1912 Alfred Wegener, a German meteorologist, revived an old observation as a grand
    claim: the continents move. His book <em>The Origin of Continents and Oceans</em> (1915)
    assembled the evidence. The coastlines fit — South America's east coast nests into
    Africa's west like torn paper, a fit later confirmed by computer at the edge of the
    continental shelf. Identical fossils — the seed fern <em>Glossopteris</em>, the
    freshwater reptile <em>Mesosaurus</em> — appear on now-distant shores but nowhere in
    between, impossible to explain by an animal swimming a salt ocean. Mountain belts match:
    the Appalachians line up with the Scottish Caledonides across the Atlantic. And glacial
    scars in bedrock appear in today's tropical India and Brazil, pointing as if ice had
    flowed outward from a shared south-polar cap that no present arrangement allows.
  </p>
  <p>
    Wegener was right about the drift and wrong about the engine — he suggested continents
    ploughed through ocean crust like ships through ice, and physicists correctly showed
    that was impossible. The rejection was near-total for half a century; then the 1950s–60s
    delivered the missing machinery. Mapping the seafloor revealed a global mountain chain
    (the Mid-Atlantic Ridge) with young rock at its crest; <strong>magnetic stripes</strong>
    recorded on either side (Vine and Matthews, 1963) showed new seafloor forming at the
    ridge, cooling through reversals of Earth's magnetic field like a tape recorder, and
    spreading symmetrically outward. Continents were not ploughing through anything: they
    ride <em>within</em> plates that are themselves gliding on the flowing mantle. Plate
    tectonics became the unifying theory of geology.
  </p>
  <p><em>Common misconception:</em> the plates are not "the continents". A plate is a slab of
  rigid lithosphere — crust <em>plus</em> the topmost mantle — up to a couple of hundred
  kilometres thick; continents ride inside plates like logos on a moving conveyor belt, and
  oceanic plates form, move and get destroyed entirely independently of them.</p>

  <h2>Plates and their three boundaries</h2>
  <p>
    Earth's rigid shell is broken into about a dozen major plates, and almost all the
    interesting geology — volcanoes, great earthquakes, mountain ranges — happens at the
    three kinds of edge between them.
  </p>
  <p>
    <strong>Divergent boundaries</strong> pull plates apart. Mantle rock rises beneath the
    thinning plate and, released from pressure, partly melts (<em>decompression
    melting</em>), welding new basaltic ocean floor onto both plates. The Mid-Atlantic Ridge
    is the slow-motion version: the Atlantic widens a couple of centimetres per year —
    roughly the rate your fingernails grow — and Iceland, straddling the ridge, is the one
    place you can walk on it. Continental rifting (the East African Rift) is the same process
    starting on land, and geologists expect it to eventually split Africa and open a new
    ocean.
  </p>
  <p>
    <strong>Convergent boundaries</strong> bring plates together, and what happens depends on
    the crust involved. Where an ocean plate meets another ocean plate, the older, denser one
    dives beneath — <em>subduction</em> — building volcanic island arcs like Japan and the
    Marianas, and the planet's deepest trench (Challenger Deep, nearly 11 km). Where ocean
    meets continent, the ocean plate subducts beneath the continent: the Peru–Chile trench
    and the Andes, whose volcanoes exist because water released from the descending slab
    lowers the melting point of the mantle above (<em>flux melting</em>) — continents are fed
    by water-driven volcanism. Where continent meets continent, neither can sink far: the
    crust crumples and thickens into the Himalaya, still rising as India continues its
    50-million-year collision with Asia.
  </p>
  <p>
    <strong>Transform boundaries</strong> slide plates past each other — no magma, no
    mountains, but enormous friction and earthquakes. The San Andreas Fault, slicing
    California, is the textbook case: the Pacific plate slips northwestward past North
    America at about 5 cm per year, in sticks and jerks that are each an earthquake.
    Transform edges are also why the famous mid-ocean ridges look like stitched zippers:
    offset by transform faults the whole way along.
  </p>
  <p><em>Common misconception:</em> the San Andreas Fault will someday drop California into
  the ocean. A transform boundary slides plates past each other — it neither opens a gap nor
  consumes crust — so the realistic future is Los Angeles creeping northward toward San
  Francisco over millions of years, earthquakes along the way, and the coastline exactly
  where plate motion leaves it.</p>

  <h2>Hot spots and mantle plumes</h2>
  <p>
    Not all volcanism sits on plate edges. Some sits on <strong>hot spots</strong>: narrow
    columns of unusually hot rock — mantle plumes — rising from deep in the mantle and
    melting the plate above from below. The evidence is that hot spots stay (nearly) fixed
    while plates glide over them, so they burn a hole a volcano at a time and leave an
    <em>age-progressive chain</em>. The Hawaiian chain is the classic: active Kīlauea sits
    over the plume, and volcano ages march steadily to the northwest along the 6,000 km
    chain, reaching tens of millions of years old at its far, eroded end — the plate's
    motion, recorded in rock. Yellowstone sits over a continental hot spot that has left a
    chain of calderas across Idaho; Iceland is a plume sitting fortuitously on a ridge,
    doubling the volcanism. Because plumes barely move, they also provide the reference
    frame against which plate motions are actually measured.
  </p>

  <h2>Check yourself</h2>
  <p>Try these before moving on. Feedback follows each answer.</p>
  <ol>
    <li>Why can't S waves be used to "see" the outer core — and what does their disappearance prove?</li>
    <li>The mantle and the asthenosphere have nearly the same composition. Why does one behave rigidly and the other plastically?</li>
    <li>Wegener had four strong lines of evidence. Name two, and explain why they were not enough.</li>
    <li>A volcano sits far from any plate boundary, over an older island already 5 million years old. What is beneath it, and which way is the plate moving?</li>
  </ol>
  <p>
    <strong>Feedback:</strong> (1) S waves are shear waves and cannot cross liquid; their
    complete disappearance beyond about 103° of arc from a quake — the shadow zone — proves
    the outer core is liquid, and the zone's width fixes the core's depth. (2) Behaviour
    depends on temperature as well as composition: asthenosphere rock sits so close to its
    melting point that it weakens and flows (seismic waves slow there), while the cooler
    lithosphere above is strong and rigid — pressure and temperature decide behaviour, not
    ingredients alone. (3) Matching coastlines, matching fossils across oceans, matching
    mountain belts, and misplaced glacial scars — any two. They were not enough because
    Wegener could not supply a mechanism: continents ploughing through solid oceanic crust
    was physically impossible, so most geologists rejected the drift. (4) It sits over a hot
    spot — a deep mantle plume melting the plate from below; the older island lies
    <em>behind</em> it in the direction the plate came from, so the plate is moving away
    from the older island's bearing (for Hawaii, toward the northwest). The chain of
    progressively older volcanoes is the plate's track record.
  </p>

  <h2>Key takeaways</h2>
  <ul>
    <li>The interior is mapped indirectly: seismic waves (P crosses liquid, S does not), reflection and tomography, density arithmetic, and meteorites as core samples.</li>
    <li>Chemical slices: crust (5–70 km, Si–Al–O), mantle (about 2,900 km, iron–magnesium peridotite), core (about 3,500 km, iron–nickel).</li>
    <li>Mechanical slices: rigid lithosphere (crust plus topmost mantle), weak asthenosphere (near its melting point, the low-velocity zone), solid mesosphere, liquid outer core, solid inner core.</li>
    <li>Pressure as well as temperature decides state: the inner core is hotter than the outer core but solid.</li>
    <li>The geotherm climbs from about 15 °C at the surface to about 5,400 °C at the centre; its kinks are mineral phase changes and its flat stretch betrays convection.</li>
    <li>Earth's heat is roughly half primordial and half radioactive decay — and it is slowly running down.</li>
    <li>Wegener's drift evidence was rejected for lack of an engine; seafloor spreading and magnetic stripes (Vine and Matthews) supplied it.</li>
    <li>Plates interact at divergent boundaries (decompression melting, new ocean floor), convergent boundaries (subduction, flux melting, mountain belts) and transform boundaries (friction and earthquakes).</li>
    <li>Hot spots are fixed plumes under moving plates, leaving age-progressive volcano chains — Hawaii is the type example.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2>Where this goes next</h2>
  <p>
    With the machine mapped, the next lessons follow its products. Volcanoes and igneous
    processes pick up where this page left magma — what melts, where, and what it becomes.
    Weathering, erosion and sedimentary rocks, then the rock cycle, follow the material the
    machine recycles; fossils and geologic time supply the deep chronology this page only
    gestured at (India's 50-million-year collision); and later units take up earthquakes,
    the water cycle and climate. The interior you met here is the engine for all of them.
  </p>

  <h2>Going deeper</h2>
  <p>
    What actually drives the plates? Textbook "convection" is only half the story. Modern
    force budgets put most of the push–pull on the plates themselves: <strong>slab pull</strong>
    — the weight of the cold, dense subducting slab dragging its plate — dominates,
    contributing perhaps 90% of the driving force, with <strong>ridge push</strong> (gravity
    sliding off the high, hot ridge crest) a secondary term. Mantle convection is therefore
    entangled with the plates: they are not cargo on a conveyor but part of the moving
    machine. Seismic tomography now images sinking slabs piled near the core–mantle boundary,
    and plumes can be traced at least partway down — though their exact depth of origin
    remains genuinely debated.
  </p>
  <p>
    The core keeps its own mysteries. The geodynamo that generates our magnetic field runs on
    convecting liquid iron, and the field's polarity <em>flips</em> — north becomes south —
    at irregular intervals averaging a few hundred thousand years (the last reversal was
    780,000 years ago). The flips are recorded in cooling lavas and spreading seafloor, which
    is precisely the "tape" Vine and Matthews read. Meanwhile the inner core is
    anisotropic — seismic waves run faster pole-to-pole than along the equator — and may
    rotate at a slightly different rate than the rest of the planet, a result that has swung
    back and forth in the literature and is still contested. Earth generates about 47
    terawatts of heat, roughly half from radioactive decay and half primordial, and that
    budget, leaking out of the floor you stand on, is the original battery behind everything
    in this lesson.
  </p>
  <p>
    The field those convecting currents make is not a decoration. It deflects the solar wind
    — the Sun's constant outflow of charged particles — carving the magnetosphere that helps
    Earth keep its atmosphere (Mars, which lost its dynamo about 4 billion years ago, lost
    much of its air afterwards, possibly not by coincidence). Charged particles funnel along
    field lines into the poles and excite the air they strike: the aurora is the magnetic
    field, made visible. Compasses, bird migration, and the magnetic stripes of the seafloor
    all read the same dynamo — one more way the planet's unvisitable interior reaches up and
    touches the surface.
  </p>
  <p><em>Common misconception:</em> the magnetic field comes from a giant bar magnet buried
  at the centre. A bar magnet would lose its magnetism at core temperatures; the field is a
  geodynamo — generated by convection of liquid iron in the outer core — which is exactly
  why it flips polarity every few hundred thousand years, something no permanent magnet
  does.</p>

